= Observations on a certain theorem of Fermat and on others concerning prime numbers by Leonhard Euler
{c}
{parent=Mathematics bibliography}
{tag=Work by Leonhard Euler}
{tag=Work by Jordan Bell}
{title2=1738}
{title2=Eneström E26}

By <Leonhard Euler>; English translation by <Jordan Bell>. Presented to the St Petersburg Academy on 26 September 1732; the original Latin paper, "Observationes de theoremate quodam Fermatiano aliisque ad numeros primos spectantibus", appeared in Commentarii academiae scientiarum Petropolitanae 6 (1738), pp. 103–107. The publication note on page one of the translation distinguishes presentation from publication. The linked translation is version three, dated 15 April 2008.

English translation: https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0501118

Catalogue record: https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0501118

Cited passages: pp. 1–2, Euler's account of Fermat's proposed prime pattern and the factor $641$ of $2^{32}+1$; p. 1 publication and translator note. The short modular check in <Fermat number> is presented as a modern verification, not a historical reconstruction of Euler's search.

= Primality testing: then and now by Carl Pomerance
{c}
{parent=Mathematics bibliography}
{tag=Work by Carl Pomerance}
{title2=2018}

By <Carl Pomerance>. Slides for the ICERM meeting "Seventy-five years of Mathematics of Computation", 1–3 November 2018.

Online text: https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/mathcomptalk2.pdf

Cited passages: slide 14 states <Pépin's test> and outlines its proof; slides 15–16 describe <Fermat numbers> and their role in <integer factorization>; slide 18 gives the <Gauss-Wantzel theorem> and distinguishes Gauss's construction result from Wantzel's necessity proof. Numerical records elsewhere in these 2018 slides are historical records, not claims about current records.

